## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Can Connect with Nature!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Relax and Listen.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>
            Reading: Summarize a text using a graphic organizer.<br>
            Reading: Follow written instructions for making or doing something simple.<br>
            Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>
            Reading: Can summarize a short text about nature using a graphic organizer by organizing key nature terms (e.g., "sun," "tree," and "bird" under the heading "Nature").<br>
            Can follow simple instructions from a nature-themed text (e.g., "Draw a leaf.").<br>
            Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words, such as blending /s/ /ʌ/ /n/ sounds to form the word "sun" when the words have been previously encountered.
            </p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a large picture of a park or a nature scene (e.g., with trees, grass, flowers, birds, sun, a river). Teacher asks, "What do you see?" and encourages students to shout out previously learned vocabulary words (e.g., "tree," "bird," "flower," "sun"). Teacher then asks, "What can you do in nature?" to activate "can" grammar. Students respond with actions like "I can walk," "I can look," "I can listen."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of "sun" and "can." Teacher clearly pronounces the words, exaggerating the /s/ sound in "sun" and the /k/ sound in "can." Teacher points to the words on the flashcards. Teacher then writes "I can see the sun" on the board and points to each word as they say it. Teacher models blending for "sun" by saying /s/ /ʌ/ /n/ then "sun."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the picture of the sun) "Is this a flower or the sun?" (Expected answer: "The sun.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing flashcard "can") "Can I fly like a bird?" (Expected answer: "No, you can't.") "Can I walk in the park?" (Expected answer: "Yes, you can.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative text (e.g., "My Day in the Park") with illustrations, printed on a worksheet or displayed on a projector. The text incorporates target vocabulary (tree, grass, flower, bird, leaf, park, sun, sky, stream, listen, look, relax, quiet, peaceful, beautiful, green) and grammar (Can/Can't, Prepositions of place, Present simple).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing connection with nature: Talking about sensory observations and experiences in nature (e.g., "I hear the birds." "The flowers smell nice."). Locating people and things: Identifying locations in nature (e.g., "I am near the big tree.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the text aloud once, pointing to words as they read. Students listen.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text silently to themselves.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "Where is the story happening?" (Expected: "In the park.") "Is the park quiet or noisy?" (Expected: "Quiet/Peaceful.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Teacher reads simple statements about the text, and students show "True" (thumbs up) or "False" (thumbs down). Examples: "I can see a red butterfly in the text." (False, if not mentioned). "I can hear birds in the park." (True, if mentioned).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness Focus:</b> Teacher points to "sun" in the text and asks students to blend the sounds after teacher's model: /s/ /ʌ/ /n/ -> "sun." Do the same for "flower": /f/ /l/ /ɑʊ/ /ɚ/ -> "flower."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying key information. Grammar: Can/Can't, Prepositions of place (in, near, under), Present simple. Vocabulary: tree, grass, flower, bird, leaf, park, sun, sky, stream, water fall, listen, look, relax, breathe, quiet, peaceful, beautiful, green.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Activity):</b> Teacher provides a worksheet with sentences from the story, but with key nature words missing. Students read the sentences and choose the correct word from a word bank (e.g., "The ____ is green." choices: 'tree', 'sun', 'grass'). Focus on words like "tree," "grass," "flower," "bird," "sun."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence/Picture Matching):</b> Teacher gives students a set of simple sentences related to the story and a set of corresponding pictures. Students read each sentence and draw a line to match it with the correct picture. Examples: "I am near the big tree." (picture of a child near a tree), "A bird sings in the sky." (picture of a bird in the sky).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Read and Answer):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small card with a simple question about the text (e.g., "What colors are the flowers?") or a true/false statement they need to confirm from the text. They read the question/statement and find the answer in the text they studied in Stage 2. Partners then orally confirm the answer. Example: "Can you see a stream in the park?" (Student A reads. Student B reads text to find answer, "Yes, I can see a peaceful stream.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual reading and graphic organizer completion, followed by simple instruction drawing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students will read a new short descriptive text about a visit to a "nature spot" (e.g., "My Favorite River Spot"). The text will use target vocabulary (river, stream, butterfly, spider, green, beautiful, peaceful) and grammar. Students will summarize the text by extracting key "nature terms" into a simple graphic organizer and follow direct instructions embedded in the text.</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading for Summary:</b> Teacher distributes a new, short nature text (e.g., "A Day by the River") and a graphic organizer with one main box labeled "My River Spot" and smaller boxes under it labeled "Nature Words" and "What I Can Do Here." Students read the text independently. They then fill in the "Nature Words" box with words from the text like "river," "butterfly," "green grass," "tree," "peaceful stream." They fill the "What I Can Do Here" box with actions like "I can relax," "I can look at butterflies," "I can hear the water."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Written Instructions:</b> The text includes simple, direct instructions at the end for students to follow. Examples: "Draw a big green leaf." "Color the butterfly blue." "Draw a small spider under the tree." Students perform these actions on their worksheet/graphic organizer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic Element:</b> While completing, teacher encourages students to say things like, "I like the peaceful river" or "This is my favorite spot" to a peer, if time allows, linking to expressing appreciation for nature.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the graphic organizer task and review of completed graphic organizers and drawings. Teacher uses a simple checklist to note if students successfully extracted key terms and followed instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their completed "My River Spot" graphic organizer with the drawings based on instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Identified at least 3-4 key nature terms from the text for the graphic organizer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Instructions:</b> Successfully completed at least 2 out of 3 drawing instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Used target nature nouns appropriately in the graphic organizer.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small paper slip:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can read and find words about ____." (e.g., "nature," "park," "river")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow instructions like 'Draw a ____'."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher self-reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new nature word did you learn today from reading?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult when reading the stories today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., engagement with pictures, clarity of instructions for the graphic organizer).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction)? (e.g., more practice with prepositions of place for speaking, visual aids for specific vocabulary during interaction).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I prepare students to actively speak and interact using these nature words in the next lesson? (e.g., practice question-answer pairs like "Where is the bird?" "It is in the tree.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The reading comprehension of nature terms and locations will be directly used in Lesson 3, where students will practice describing their observations and locations in nature using speaking and interaction activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>